# Lab 03 -- act configuration (starter)
#
# TODO(lab-03-f): Right now the only way to find out whether a workflow change
# works is to commit, push, and wait. That is a feedback loop measured in
# minutes for a class of mistake -- a typo in a shell line, a wrong path, a
# missing `shell:` -- that a local run catches in seconds.
#
# `act` runs workflow jobs in Docker containers on your laptop. Configure it
# here so `make ci-local` needs no flags. You need three settings:
#
#   1. Map ubuntu-latest to a runner image that actually has tools installed.
#      act's default is a near-empty stub and almost nothing will run on it.
#      Use: -P ubuntu-latest=catthehacker/ubuntu:act-latest
#
#   2. On Apple Silicon, force the container architecture to linux/amd64.
#      Slower under emulation, but it matches what GitHub runs.
#
#   3. Give it an artifact server path, or any step using upload-artifact
#      fails outright instead of being skipped.
#
# Then run:  make ci-local JOB=api
#
# Know what act cannot do before you trust it: OIDC token minting, most of the
# `github` context, the real Actions cache backend, and GitHub-hosted service
# container networking are all approximated or absent. act catches wiring
# mistakes. Only a real run proves a workflow works.

# act's default runner image is a near-empty stub. This one ships the tools a
# GitHub runner has, so `make install` and the setup actions have something to
# work with.
-P ubuntu-latest=catthehacker/ubuntu:act-latest

# The runner images are built for amd64. On Apple Silicon, Docker would
# otherwise pick an arm64 image that does not exist, or run one that behaves
# differently from CI. Emulated and slow beats fast and unrepresentative.
--container-architecture linux/amd64

# actions/upload-artifact needs somewhere to write. Without this the step
# fails outright rather than being skipped, so a job that uploads coverage
# cannot be run locally at all.
--artifact-server-path /tmp/act-artifacts
